---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('postscript')!;
const b = appBySlug('sendlane')!;
const path = '/compare/postscript-vs-sendlane';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/postscript-vs-sendlane');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/postscript-vs-sendlane');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Postscript or Sendlane for cart recovery?",
    "answer": "Shortlist Postscript for an SMS-led Shopify program and Sendlane for a combined email/SMS evaluation. Decide from the scope offered to your account and demonstrated recovery behavior, rather than assuming either has exclusive channel coverage."
  },
  {
    "question": "What changed in the product comparison?",
    "answer": "Postscript now advertises email as well as SMS. Sendlane announces that it has joined Privy. Ask which products, migration services and support terms are actually included in a new agreement."
  },
  {
    "question": "When should a cart message be sent?",
    "answer": "Set delays around your buying cycle and permitted sending windows. Test purchase cancellation at the actual send boundary; a fixed fifteen-minute exit promise is not established here."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should recovery be measured?",
    "answer": "Compare incremental contribution using a suitably designed holdout and consistent order accounting. Platform-attributed revenue alone does not establish that a message caused an order."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should migration work?",
    "answer": "Assign each live trigger and channel one sender. Transfer permissions and exclusions, validate with non-sending tests, then retire old queues before activating replacements. Keep a rollback owner."
  },
  {
    "question": "How does a discount affect margin?",
    "answer": "Hypothetically, a $780 cart at 48% gross margin contributes $374.40 before other costs. A 12% discount removes $93.60 of revenue, leaving $280.80 when product cost stays fixed."
  },
  {
    "question": "What should a quote include?",
    "answer": "Request channel charges, platform fees, sending and contact allowances, overages, onboarding, support, contract terms and exit/export arrangements for the same workload."
  },
  {
    "question": "What must a demo prove?",
    "answer": "Require cart identity, channel eligibility, purchase cancellation, replay deduplication, offer handling and auditable reporting using your proposed acceptance cases. This page reports no firsthand product test."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/compare" class="link text-sm font-bold">← Comparisons</a>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">For a retention team deciding whether to keep specialist channel ownership or consolidate email and SMS: compare the actual migration scope, recovery controls and operating workload.</p>
      <div class="mt-10">
        <TldrBox picks={[{ label: 'SMS-led shortlist', tool: a.name, reason: 'Evaluate Shopify SMS workflows alongside your existing email operation.' }, { label: 'Combined shortlist', tool: b.name, reason: 'Evaluate email and SMS together, including the current Privy relationship.' }]} />
      </div>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Start with the scope being sold today</h2>
        <p><a href="https://postscript.io/features" class="link">Postscript’s feature page</a> describes Shopify-focused SMS automation, including checkout and order triggers; its navigation also advertises email. <a href="https://www.sendlane.com/" class="link">Sendlane</a> markets email, SMS, forms and multichannel automation. Its <a href="https://www.sendlane.com/blog/privy-acquires-sendlane" class="link">Privy acquisition announcement</a> makes the current product and contracting scope a necessary part of evaluation. Sources checked November 24, 2026. These are supplier descriptions, not independent performance results.</p>
        <p>The practical question is which work you want to move. A successful email program may be worth retaining while evaluating an SMS workflow. A fragmented retention operation may justify a broader migration, provided consolidation actually removes work. Neither outcome follows from the channel labels alone. Ask each supplier to identify the exact products offered, availability for your countries and account, and which team owns onboarding and support.</p>
        <div class="card overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Keep separate ownership</th><th>Consolidate the program</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Migration surface</td><td>Prove the new channel works with existing email queues.</td><td>Rebuild email and SMS triggers, content, exclusions and reporting.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Operator responsibility</td><td>Name an owner for conflicts between senders.</td><td>Prove one operator can trace a customer across channels.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Offer ownership</td><td>Coordinate welcome and cart offers across tools.</td><td>Demonstrate shared offer rules rather than assuming them.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Commercial scope</td><td>Include retained software and coordination costs.</td><td>Include migration effort and every contracted channel.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <h2>Map the recovery journey before rebuilding it</h2>
        <p>Export a trigger register: abandoned checkout, browse reminder, welcome incentive, back-in-stock and post-purchase. Record the current sender, eligibility rule, delay, exit event, offer and reporting owner for each. Cart and checkout events should remain distinguishable; a phone number in a checkout does not by itself establish marketing eligibility.</p>
        <p>For the split-channel option, ask the operator to trace a shopper who has an email reminder queued when an SMS branch becomes eligible. Show how the combined contact policy is enforced and where the operator sees the decision. For consolidation, trace the same journey in the proposed account and ask what data was actually unified. A shared invoice is not proof of a shared suppression state.</p>
        <h2>Proposed acceptance cases for the demo</h2>
        <p>These are evaluation cases to run with safe test contacts, not tests we have performed. Save the event payload, eligibility decision, queue state and final action for each case so a failure has an owner.</p>
        <h3>One shopper, different permissions</h3>
        <p>Use an email-eligible shopper without SMS permission, then an SMS-eligible shopper without email permission. Check the permitted path in each proposed architecture. Repeat after withdrawal and after importing a historical suppression. Preserve the source and scope of permission; a generic “TCPA opted” field does not describe every jurisdiction, channel or use.</p>
        <h3>A purchase arrives while a message waits</h3>
        <p>Place a test order after entry, then near the dispatch boundary. Inspect whether queued recovery stops, whether the purchase matches the right checkout, and how event delay is handled. Agree an operational tolerance based on measured behavior and escalation procedures. Do not treat fifteen minutes as a supplier guarantee. A later refund needs a separate business rule; it should not automatically restart an abandoned-cart offer.</p>
        <h3>A checkout update is delivered twice</h3>
        <p>Replay the same event and then change a variant in the cart. Determine whether the workflow deduplicates or starts a new journey, and whether the recovery link still resolves to the intended items. Keep a record of the identifiers used. This matters especially during cutover, when an old integration and a new connection may both receive the same activity.</p>
        <h3>The offer already exists elsewhere</h3>
        <p>Use a cart with a welcome code and an automatic bundle discount. Check redemption restrictions, combinations, expiry and the offer shown in each channel. Assign responsibility for generating and retiring codes. If email and SMS promote different incentives, decide which promise the support team will honor before a customer encounters the conflict.</p>
        <h3>The cart cannot be purchased as written</h3>
        <p>Change stock for one variant, then make the whole cart unavailable. Inspect the message and destination immediately before sending. An assistance message or stock notification may be more appropriate than a recovery coupon. For a subscription cart, also inspect whether the link and incentive preserve the intended purchase option.</p>
        <h2>Use revenue discounts with fixed product costs</h2>
        <p>Hypothetical example: a $780 cart at 48% gross margin has $405.60 product cost and $374.40 full-price contribution. A 12% code removes $93.60 from revenue. Discounted revenue is $686.40; contribution is $280.80 before shipping, payment fees, returns, messaging and other costs. Multiplying the original contribution by 88% would incorrectly reduce product cost as well.</p>
        <p>The discounted order retains 75% of the original contribution, so matching full-price contribution requires about 33.33% more orders under these simplified assumptions. That is a break-even arithmetic comparison, not a forecast that either supplier will produce that lift. Model the actual mix of full-price and discounted orders, not a blanket discount on every recovered cart.</p>
        <p>For a second hypothetical cart, $430 at 69% margin means $133.30 product cost and $296.70 contribution. A 13% code removes $55.90 and leaves $240.80. Use the lower contribution when evaluating extra message charges. A message that receives attribution for an order that would have happened anyway can reduce profit even if the recovery dashboard looks strong.</p>
        <h2>Separate attribution from the business case</h2>
        <p>Agree on order identifiers, attribution windows, refunds, discount allocation and how both channels are deduplicated. Use Shopify order records as the reconciliation base. If using a holdout, choose its size and duration from traffic, expected effect and statistical requirements; this page does not prescribe a universal percentage. Include all eligible shoppers assigned to each group, rather than comparing only recipients who clicked.</p>
        <p>Request matched quotes for the same list, email volume, SMS destinations, message composition and peak-season workload. Include retained tools in the split option and onboarding, creative rebuilds, data cleanup and staff time in the consolidation option. Ask who resolves an event delivery problem and whether support commitments and migration assistance are contractual. Do not substitute an old starting price or a marketplace rating for this analysis.</p>
        <h2>Cut over by ownership, with a rollback path</h2>
        <p>Validate imports, links and exclusions without sending. Keep one live sender for each trigger and channel, retire its pending queues, then activate the replacement under an assigned owner. Account for messages already handed to a delivery provider. Keep the old configuration documented for rollback, but prevent it from reactivating automatically or replaying historical carts.</p>
        <p>Choose a limited initial cohort that your team can inspect, with explicit stop conditions for duplicate sends, eligibility failures and broken destinations. Expand only after reconciling events, orders and support issues. The right duration depends on event volume and buying cycle; a fixed two-week overlap is not a safe substitute for queue control.</p>
        <h2>Make the final choice from demonstrated work</h2>
        <p>Keep a channel specialist on the shortlist when the existing email operation is valuable and your team can own cross-tool coordination. Favor a broader consolidation evaluation when duplicated data work and fragmented ownership are the main problem. If either demo cannot trace exclusions and purchases through the proposed workflow, resolve that gap before comparing promised revenue gains. Current plan availability, integration behavior, legal requirements and contract terms remain account-specific questions.</p>
      </article>
      <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title={`${a.name} vs ${b.name} FAQ`} />
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
